    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Pick poured concrete if you want the lowest upfront cost for a large, simple patio or driveway and can live with the near certainty of some cracking, because plain concrete typically costs roughly half as much as pavers installed. Pick pavers if you are staying long term, live with hard freeze-thaw winters, or care about appearance and repairability, because individual units can be lifted and reset instead of breaking out a slab, and well-built paver surfaces often outlast concrete. Stamped concrete sits in the middle: paver-like looks at a price that can approach pavers, with concrete's cracking risk. The sections below compare cost, cracking, repairs, climate, maintenance, lifespan and resale so you can decide for your own yard.
        </p>

        <div id="side-by-side" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Do Pavers and Concrete Compare?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Poured concrete is a single monolithic slab placed wet, finished and left to cure. Interlocking pavers are individual units, usually manufactured concrete, clay brick or natural stone, laid tightly over a compacted gravel base and a thin layer of bedding sand, with joint sand swept between them. Both make durable patios, walkways and driveways. They fail differently, cost differently and age differently, and that is what decides the right choice.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Factor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Poured Concrete</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Concrete Pavers</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical installed cost (patio)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $6-12 per sq ft plain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $12-25+ per sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical installed cost (driveway)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $6-15 per sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly $15-30+ per sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cracking</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Expected; control joints guide it</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Joints absorb movement; units rarely crack</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Repairs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Patches show; panels may need replacing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lift and reset or replace individual units</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Freeze-thaw</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Vulnerable to scaling and cracking</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flexible; dense units resist frost well</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Maintenance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fill cracks, seal every few years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Top up joint sand, control weeds, optional sealing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical lifespan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Around 25-30+ years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often 30-50+ years with upkeep</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Look</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plain, colored, stained or stamped</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wide range of shapes, colors and patterns</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            These figures are typical US ranges, not quotes. Regional labor rates, site prep, the style of paver and the complexity of the pattern all move the numbers, and prices change from year to year. The direction of each comparison is consistent, though: concrete is cheaper and faster to install, pavers cost more and take longer but handle movement better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manufactured concrete pavers made to the common ASTM C936 standard are required to reach a high average compressive strength, around 8,000 psi, and low water absorption. Typical residential poured concrete is often in the 3,000 to 4,000 psi range or a little higher for exterior work. That does not make pavers indestructible, but it explains why individual units hold up so well to traffic, frost and salt.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are leaning toward a poured patio and want a detailed budget, our <a href="/concrete-patio-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete patio cost</a> guide breaks down prices by size and finish, including base prep, removal of an old slab and sealing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Are Pavers More Expensive Than Concrete?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, usually by a wide margin up front. A plain broom-finished concrete patio commonly runs roughly $6 to $12 per square foot installed, while a concrete paver patio commonly runs roughly $12 to $25 or more. For driveways, which need thicker pavers and a deeper base, pavers commonly cost roughly $15 to $30 or more per square foot, compared with roughly $6 to $15 for poured concrete. Clay brick and natural stone pavers cost more again.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason is labor. Concrete is placed and finished as a single slab, often in a day. Pavers require excavation, a thicker compacted base, a screeded sand bed, hand-laying every unit, cutting edges, installing edge restraints, compacting the surface and sweeping in joint sand. A large paver driveway can take a crew several days.
          </p>

          <p>
            Over the long run, the gap narrows. Concrete that cracks badly or settles may need panels replaced, which is expensive and rarely matches the original. Pavers that settle can be lifted, the base corrected, and the same units relaid. If you plan to stay 20 or 30 years, the lifetime cost of pavers can approach or even beat concrete, especially in harsh climates. If you are selling in five years, concrete's lower upfront cost is hard to argue with.
          </p>

          <p>
            Driveways deserve a specific note. A paver driveway needs pavers rated for vehicles, commonly about 80 mm (3 1/8 inch) thick rather than the 60 mm (2 3/8 inch) units often used on patios, and a deeper compacted base. A paver driveway installed with patio-grade units or a shallow base will rut and shift. Make sure any driveway quote specifies vehicular pavers and base depth. For a broader look at driveway options, see our <a href="/asphalt-vs-concrete-driveway/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">asphalt vs concrete driveway</a> comparison.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cracking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which One Cracks Less and Is Easier to Repair?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete cracks. That is not a sign of bad work; it is what concrete does as it shrinks while curing and moves with temperature and soil. Good contractors cut or tool control joints so the cracks form neatly in the joints, but random cracks still appear over time, especially where the base was weak, roots grow under the slab or water collects. Most concrete patios and driveways have at least a few cracks within their first several years.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pavers handle movement differently. The joints between units act as thousands of tiny control joints, letting the surface flex slightly as the ground freezes, thaws or settles. Individual pavers rarely crack under normal use. When the base settles, the surface dips or shifts instead, which is usually easier to see and to fix than a cracked slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repairs are where pavers clearly win. A sunken area can be lifted, the bedding sand and base corrected, and the same pavers reset, often leaving no trace. A stained or broken unit can be swapped. Utility work under a paver walk can be done without saws. With concrete, a crack can be filled, but the repair usually shows, and a badly cracked or settled panel may need to be leveled or cut out and repoured, which rarely matches the surrounding slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a catch with pavers. A poor base causes settling and ruts, and missing or weak edge restraints let the units creep apart. The quality of a paver installation is mostly hidden underground, so the base depth, compaction and edge restraint matter more than the paver you choose. A cheap paver job can perform worse than well-built concrete.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Concrete cracks; pavers shift</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Expect cracks in concrete and plan to fill them. Expect some settling in pavers and plan to reset the low spots. The repair you are more willing to live with is a good guide to which surface fits you.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="climate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Holds Up Better to Freeze-Thaw and Road Salt?
          </h2>

          <p>
            In cold climates, pavers usually have the edge. Freeze-thaw cycles lift and drop the ground, and a rigid slab responds by cracking, while a flexible paver surface moves with it. Dense manufactured pavers also absorb little water, which limits the internal freezing that causes surface damage. That combination is why pavers are popular in regions with harsh winters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete can handle freeze-thaw well too, if it is built right. Exterior concrete in cold climates should generally be air-entrained, which means it contains tiny, evenly distributed air bubbles that give freezing water room to expand. Properly cured, air-entrained concrete with good drainage can last decades in cold regions. Poorly cured or non-air-entrained concrete is far more likely to scale, flake and spall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deicing salts are hard on both surfaces, but concrete tends to show it more, especially when it is young. Salts increase the number of freeze-thaw cycles and can contribute to scaling. Many concrete guides recommend avoiding deicing salts on concrete during its first winter. Pavers can show surface wear and efflorescence from salt as well, but a damaged unit can be replaced, while a scaled slab usually needs resurfacing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Snow removal is one area where concrete wins. A smooth slab is easy to shovel, snowblow and plow. Pavers can catch a plow blade if any units have settled or lifted, and joint sand can be disturbed by repeated plowing. If you use a plow service, tell them the surface is pavers and ask about blade shoes or a rubber edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            In hot, dry climates without freezing, the climate argument mostly goes away. Concrete is less stressed, cracking from frost is not a factor, and the decision comes down to cost, look and repairability. Surface temperature can matter on patios in strong sun, where lighter colors of either material stay cooler underfoot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Needs More Maintenance?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete is lower maintenance day to day. Sweep it, wash it occasionally, fill cracks as they appear, and seal it every few years if you want to protect it from water, salt and stains. A penetrating sealer on plain concrete can last several years between applications, while film-forming sealers on stamped or colored concrete usually need recoating more often.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pavers need a bit more attention. Joint sand washes out over time, especially with pressure washing, and should be topped up to keep the units locked together. Polymeric sand, which hardens after it is wetted, reduces washout, weeds and ant activity compared with plain sand, though it still wears and needs replacing eventually. Our picks for the <a href="/best-polymeric-sand-for-pavers/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best polymeric sand for pavers</a> cover joint widths and setting times.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealing is optional for both, but common for pavers. A paver sealer can enhance color, help stabilize joint sand, and make stains easier to clean. Many owners reseal every few years. Our <a href="/do-pavers-need-to-be-sealed/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">do pavers need to be sealed</a> guide explains when it pays and when it is wasted money, and our tested <a href="/best-paver-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best paver sealer</a> picks cover both natural and wet-look finishes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weeds are the most common complaint about pavers. Seeds land in the joints and sprout, especially where joint sand is low or organic debris collects. Keeping joints full, sweeping regularly and using polymeric sand cut this down. Concrete has fewer places for weeds to grow, though cracks and control joints will eventually host a few.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stains affect both. Oil, rust and leaf tannins stain concrete and pavers alike. On concrete, a stubborn stain can be permanent or need a cleaner and a lot of scrubbing. On pavers, the worst-stained units can simply be pulled and replaced, which is one more point for repairability, provided you kept a few spare pavers from the original job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stamped" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Stamped Concrete a Good Middle Ground?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stamped concrete is poured concrete that is colored and pressed with textured mats while still workable, so it looks like brick, slate, stone or pavers. It gives you a decorative surface in a single slab, often at a lower cost than a comparable paver installation. Typical installed prices run roughly $12 to $25 per square foot, which overlaps the lower end of paver pricing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is that stamped concrete is still concrete. It cracks the same way plain concrete does, and a crack running through a fake grout line or across a stone pattern can be more noticeable than one in plain gray. Repairs are harder to hide because matching color and pattern on a patch is difficult. A paver surface that settles can be reset; a stamped slab that cracks usually stays cracked.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stamped concrete also needs more regular sealing than plain concrete to protect its color and surface. Most installers recommend resealing every two to three years or so with a film-forming sealer, which can become slippery when wet if no anti-slip additive is used. Our <a href="/best-stamped-concrete-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best stamped concrete sealer</a> roundup covers products that protect color without turning the patio into a skating rink.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where stamped concrete makes the most sense is a patio or pool deck in a mild climate, where you want a decorative look on a budget and cracking from frost is less of a concern. In a hard-freeze climate, or for a driveway carrying heavy vehicles, many owners find pavers a better long-term choice for a decorative surface, even at a higher upfront price.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more consideration: style. Stamped concrete patterns come in and out of fashion and can look dated, while real pavers, brick and natural stone tend to age more gracefully. That is a matter of taste, but it is worth looking at a few stamped patios that are ten years old before committing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. So Which Should You Choose?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choose plain poured concrete if budget is your top priority, the area is large and simple, you live in a mild climate or will use air-entrained concrete in a cold one, and you value low day-to-day upkeep. It is the best value per square foot for a functional patio or driveway, and with good base prep, joints and curing it can last decades.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose pavers if you plan to stay long term, live where winters are hard, want the flexibility to repair sections without visible patches, or care about appearance and design options. Pavers cost more upfront, but their repairability and freeze-thaw tolerance often make them the better investment over 20 or more years, particularly for driveways in cold climates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose stamped concrete if you want a decorative look on a tighter budget, in a mild climate, and accept that cracks and resealing come with it. For resale, any well-maintained surface helps curb appeal more than the material itself; buyers notice cracked, stained or uneven surfaces far more than they notice which material you chose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, the hidden parts decide the outcome. For concrete, that means a compacted base, proper slope, control joints, the right mix and good curing. For pavers, it means base depth and compaction, edge restraints, the right paver thickness for the load and full joints. Ask every contractor about those details, and you will get a surface that lasts regardless of the material.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using patio pavers on a driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Driveways need pavers rated for vehicles, commonly about 80 mm thick, over a deeper compacted base than a patio. Thin patio pavers on a shallow base will rut, crack and shift under cars. Confirm the paver thickness and base depth in writing before hiring anyone for a paver driveway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skimping on the paver base</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The base is most of the cost and most of the performance of a paver surface. A thin or poorly compacted base settles, leaving dips and ruts, and edge restraints that are missing or weak let pavers drift apart. Ask how deep the base will be, what material is used and how it is compacted.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pouring non-air-entrained concrete in a cold climate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Exterior concrete in freeze-thaw climates should generally be air-entrained to resist scaling. Without it, the surface is far more likely to flake and spall after a few winters, especially if deicing salt is used. Ask your contractor which mix they plan to order before they pour.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure washing joint sand away</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Aggressive pressure washing blasts joint sand out of paver joints, loosening the units and inviting weeds. Use a wide fan tip held at a distance, then refill the joints with sand or polymeric sand afterward. If you plan to seal, let the joints set fully according to the sand maker's instructions first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing stamped concrete expecting no cracks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stamped concrete is still concrete, and it cracks like any slab. Cracks across a decorative pattern can be more noticeable, and patching rarely matches color and texture. If cracking would bother you, choose pavers, or accept that the stamped surface will need crack repair and resealing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do pavers add more value than concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A well-built paver patio or driveway can boost curb appeal more than plain concrete, and many buyers view pavers as an upgrade. But no hardscape returns its full cost at sale, and condition matters more than material. A cracked or stained surface hurts appeal either way. Choose based on your own use and budget rather than expected resale gain.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are pavers or concrete better for a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Concrete is cheaper up front and easier to plow. Pavers cost more but handle freeze-thaw movement better and can be repaired one section at a time. In cold climates, a properly built paver driveway with vehicular-grade units and a deep base often outlasts concrete. In mild climates on a budget, air-entrained poured concrete is a strong, economical choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you lay pavers over existing concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes. If the slab is sound, level and drains well, thin pavers can be installed over it with a bedding layer and proper drainage at the edges. Cracked or heaving slabs make poor bases, since their movement will show through. Raising the surface height can also cause problems at doors and steps. Many installers prefer to remove the slab and build a proper base.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do pavers last compared to concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Concrete paver surfaces built on a proper base commonly last 30 to 50 years or more, partly because damaged units can be replaced. Poured concrete commonly lasts 25 to 30 years or more before it needs major repair or replacement, depending on climate, base and care. In both cases, base preparation and drainage are the biggest factors in reaching the long end.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do pavers need to be sealed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, sealing is optional. Manufactured pavers are dense and durable without it. Sealing can enhance color, help stabilize joint sand and make stains easier to clean, which is why many owners do it every few years. If you prefer the natural look and keep joints filled, unsealed pavers perform well. Poured concrete benefits more from sealing in freeze-thaw climates with salt.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is stamped concrete cheaper than pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, but not always by much. Stamped concrete typically runs roughly $12 to $25 per square foot installed, which overlaps the lower end of paver pricing. Complex patterns, multiple colors and borders push stamped toward paver prices. Over the long term, stamped concrete needs more frequent sealing and cracks cannot be reset, so the lifetime cost gap is often smaller than the upfront one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do weeds grow between pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, weeds can sprout in paver joints, especially when joint sand is low or organic debris collects. Keeping joints full, sweeping regularly and using polymeric sand, which hardens when wetted, greatly reduces weeds. Sealing can also help stabilize joints. Concrete has fewer gaps for weeds, though cracks and control joints eventually collect some soil and seeds.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

