        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Concrete is the budget choice: cheaper to install, easy to clean and plow, and long-lasting when built on a good base with the right mix. Pavers are the long-term choice: more expensive up front, but flexible in freeze-thaw climates, repairable one piece at a time and often longer-lived. Stamped concrete splits the difference on looks but not on cracking.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Decide based on how long you will stay, how hard your winters are, and how much cracks or settling would bother you. Then focus on the parts you cannot see: the base, the drainage, the joints and the installer. Those decide whether either surface gives you decades of service.
          </p>
